						const body = "\nStarting a strength training program is one of the most impactful decisions you can make for your long-term health. Research consistently shows that resistance training improves muscle mass, bone density, insulin sensitivity, cardiovascular health, and even cognitive function.\n\nBut the gym can feel overwhelming. Which exercises should you do? How many sets and reps? How heavy should you lift? This guide answers all of these questions with a science-backed foundation.\n\n---\n\n## Why Strength Training Works: The Core Principle\n\nEverything in strength training revolves around one fundamental concept: **Progressive Overload**.\n\nProgressive overload is the systematic increase of stress placed upon the musculoskeletal system over time. When a muscle is challenged beyond its current capacity, it responds by adapting — becoming stronger and larger to handle that stress in the future.\n\nWithout progressive overload, adaptation stops. This is why people who do the same workout with the same weights for years see little to no improvement.\n\nThere are four primary ways to apply progressive overload:\n1. **Increase the weight** lifted (adding plates, using heavier dumbbells)\n2. **Increase the reps** performed with the same weight\n3. **Increase the sets** performed in a workout\n4. **Decrease rest time** between sets (making work density higher)\n\nFor beginners, simply adding 2.5 kg (5 lbs) to the bar on your key lifts each week is the most effective and sustainable strategy.\n\n---\n\n## The Big Four: Compound Movements Every Beginner Must Learn\n\nCompound exercises engage multiple muscle groups and joints simultaneously. They are the cornerstone of any effective strength training program because they recruit the most muscle tissue, stimulate the greatest hormonal response, and allow you to use the heaviest loads.\n\n### 1. The Squat — \"The King of Exercises\"\nThe barbell back squat (or goblet squat for beginners) primarily targets the quadriceps, glutes, and hamstrings, while secondarily engaging the core, spinal erectors, and calves.\n\n**Why it matters:** No other movement builds lower body mass and functional strength as effectively as the squat. It mirrors the fundamental human movement of sitting and standing.\n\n**Key cues:** Feet shoulder-width apart, chest up, knees tracking over toes, hip crease below knee at the bottom.\n\n### 2. The Deadlift — The Full-Body Strength Standard\nThe conventional deadlift works the hamstrings, glutes, lower back, traps, and grip strength. It is the most effective single exercise for building posterior chain strength.\n\n**Why it matters:** The deadlift trains the entire body to generate force from the floor — a uniquely functional pattern that translates to real-world strength.\n\n**Key cues:** Bar over mid-foot, hip-hinge to reach the bar, flat back, drive through the floor.\n\n### 3. The Bench Press — Upper Body Power\nThe flat barbell bench press targets the pectorals, anterior deltoids, and triceps. It is the gold standard for measuring upper body pushing strength.\n\n**Why it matters:** Developing chest and shoulder pressing strength improves posture and builds a foundation for athletic movements.\n\n**Key cues:** Retract and depress shoulder blades, slight arch in lower back, bar path to lower chest, full range of motion.\n\n### 4. The Overhead Press (OHP) — Shoulder Development\nThe barbell or dumbbell overhead press targets the deltoids and triceps while requiring total-body tension and core stability.\n\n**Why it matters:** Pressing overhead is a fundamental human movement pattern and a critical indicator of shoulder health. A strong OHP protects the shoulder joint.\n\n**Key cues:** Bar starts at collarbone height, core and glutes braced, elbows slightly forward, press straight up.\n\n---\n\n## Sets, Reps, and Load: A Beginner's Framework\n\nFor beginners, the rep range and set structure that maximize both strength and muscle gain:\n\n| Goal | Rep Range | Sets | Rest |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Strength | 3–5 reps | 4–6 | 3–5 min |\n| Muscle Growth (Hypertrophy) | 6–12 reps | 3–4 | 60–90 sec |\n| Endurance | 15–20+ reps | 2–3 | 30–60 sec |\n\n**For most beginners, the sweet spot is 3 sets of 8–12 reps.** This range provides sufficient mechanical tension (heavy enough to challenge the muscle) and metabolic stress (enough volume to stimulate growth).\n\n**Starting weight:** Choose a weight you can lift with perfect form for 12 reps but that feels genuinely challenging by rep 10. This is approximately 60–70% of your one-rep maximum (1RM).\n\n---\n\n## A Simple 3-Day Full-Body Program (Weeks 1–8)\n\nThis is a time-tested full-body split, performed Monday, Wednesday, and Friday (or any 3 non-consecutive days):\n\n### Day A\n1. Barbell Back Squat — 3 × 8\n2. Barbell Bench Press — 3 × 8\n3. Barbell Row — 3 × 8\n4. Dumbbell Overhead Press — 2 × 12\n5. Plank — 3 × 30 seconds\n\n### Day B\n1. Romanian Deadlift — 3 × 8\n2. Incline Dumbbell Press — 3 × 10\n3. Lat Pulldown / Pull-up — 3 × 8–10\n4. Dumbbell Lunges — 2 × 10 each leg\n5. Cable Row — 2 × 12\n\nAlternate Days A and B each week. Add 2.5 kg (5 lbs) to each compound lift when you can complete all reps with good form.\n\n---\n\n## Nutrition: The Other Half of the Equation\n\nStrength training creates the stimulus for muscle growth, but **nutrition provides the raw materials**.\n\n- **Protein:** Aim for 1.6–2.2 grams per kg of body weight. This is non-negotiable for muscle repair and growth.\n- **Calories:** You must eat at or slightly above maintenance (100–300 calorie surplus) to build muscle efficiently.\n- **Carbohydrates:** Carbs fuel your workouts and replenish muscle glycogen. Do not fear them.\n- **Sleep:** 7–9 hours of quality sleep per night is when the vast majority of muscle protein synthesis occurs.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Key Principles\n- **Progressive overload** is the mechanism behind all strength and muscle gains.\n- Focus on **compound movements**: Squat, Deadlift, Bench Press, Overhead Press.\n- Train **3 days per week** with a full-body program for the first 8–12 weeks.\n- Aim for **3 sets of 8–12 reps** with challenging weight.\n- Support your training with **adequate protein (1.6–2.2g/kg)** and a slight caloric surplus.\n\n---\n\n## 🧮 Calculate Your Training Nutrition Targets\n\nUse these free calculators to set your calorie and protein targets before your first session:\n\n👉 [TDEE Calculator](/calculator/tdee)\n👉 [Protein Calculator](/calculator/protein)\n";
